      "claimLabel": "custodian: software token on the MX95 board (board-local, phase 1; no KMIP); data owner on the Mac; FHE server on the KV260 (untrusted compute)",
      "notes": "Custodian: the MX95 board (6x Cortex-A55, 8 GB, glibc 2.43) ran the token from a clean aarch64 release build of pqctoday-hsm a0a60b69 (sha256 0d7f4d82…e749); the appliance's own services were untouched. Token setup took 1 s; the export took about 5 s, including the 30,147,061 B compressed server key, the 33,034 B compact public key and two ML-DSA-65 signatures. The KV260 checked the pinned signer, key identity, parameter hash, SHA-384 and signature in 298 ms and refused 3 of 3 bad keys. 7 of 7 results reached the token: 6 released correctly, FheUint64 refused by policy, about 0.45 s each including SSH. The owner reached the MX95 board over the owner's management network over SSH (phase-1 exception; crypto-network-only remains the rule) and the KV260 over the compute board's own network over SSH; the two boards never talked to each other. The signer pin was taken from the export, fetched over the owner's own session; the token's signer attestation was not checked. An earlier attempt the same day is not evidence: a script fault let only 1 of 7 results reach the token; it was fixed in 77d3b29 and the whole flow repeated with a fresh token. The MX95 token's memory was not measured. The run summary is withheld because it names internal hosts; its facts are given here. Journal files are published redacted: the board's host name is replaced by \"kv260-board\", byte for byte otherwise. Original: journal.txt (original sha256 10f403ec1635cf77341cbea9e138b567136b83e300b8d875daa97ab344cd5e57).",
